## An evaluation guide checks model judges against human labels

Source: Machine Learning Mastery — https://machinelearningmastery.com/the-complete-guide-to-llm-evaluation/
Published: 2026-11-13
Channel: hn

Machine Learning Mastery’s guide describes evaluation as a repeatable way to compare language-model applications using test inputs, scoring rules, and a history of results. Reading a few plausible answers can miss regressions after a prompt, model, or retrieval change. The guide distinguishes quality comparisons from binary checks that protect a release, such as whether an answer has valid structure.

First, use ordinary code for criteria that have clear rules. For subjective judgments, give a model a written rubric, the evidence it may use, and a strict output format. Then label a sample of outputs by hand and compare those labels with the judge’s decisions. The guide recommends an agreement statistic that discounts matches expected by chance, because a judge that always passes answers can look accurate on an easy dataset.

For comparisons between two answers, swap their positions and inspect disagreements. For agents, check whether the task was completed before diagnosing tool choice, arguments, and unnecessary loops. Finally, rerun the judge’s labeled test set when its prompt or underlying model changes; calibration is itself a regression-testing task.

Why it matters: Engineers relying on model judges should maintain human-labeled checks for the judge itself and investigate disagreement before treating a score as a release signal.

